cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or the accompanying preprint, please cite the work via the Zenodo concept DOI."
title: "multiscale-governance-descriptors: Multi-Scale Structural Descriptors for Governance-Relevant Patterns in Data Lineage Graphs"
abstract: >-
  Python implementation, preprint source, and replication artifacts for a study
  of how much governance signal is recoverable from the topology of data lineage
  graphs alone. Four descriptor families are computed over directed acyclic
  lineage graphs, covering community stability under a Louvain resolution sweep,
  blast-radius concentration, spectral structure from the combinatorial
  Laplacian, and persistent first-homology path redundancy. The headline result
  is negative. A domain-level correlation between the spectral-gap descriptor
  and documentation rate (Spearman rho = -0.708, n = 18) collapses under a
  layer-stratified permutation test at p = 1.000, because the descriptor is
  constant within each architectural layer stratum. The association is
  between-layer architecture rather than within-layer governance quality. A
  node-level core-asset prediction on the six DLG-DG-23 graphs reaches mean
  leave-one-graph-out logistic-regression AUC = 0.898 +/- 0.098 against a random
  baseline of 0.546, but that reproduces a published centrality finding on the
  same data and does not evaluate the four descriptor families. The portable
  contribution is the inference protocol for small-sample, rank-degenerate
  graph-correlation studies that establishes the negative result.
